    "turn_idx": 50,
    "turn_text": "give her credit for. And she's an object of scorn and ridicule on the right and an object of fascination on the left. But she has continued to make moves that surprise me as an observer, confound me sometimes. Her involvement in the Democratic primary in Massachusetts, where she's siding again with the old guy, Ed Markey, over a Kennedy in a race where Joe Kennedy is trying to claim a mantle of generational change, she's again proving loyalty. You're saying, look, someone who is as good on the issues that we care about, including the Green New Deal as Ed Markey, the Senator in Massachusetts. deserves to be re-elected and shouldn't be defeated just because someone is a couple of decades younger. I think she's making some really interesting moves and I guarantee that this move around Bernie Sanders was not by accident. The timing of it can't be by accident either. This is a time where Bernie needs to get a boost. And I'll tell you, a lot of progressives I've talked to in the last couple of weeks, they're legitimately concerned perhaps about Bernie Sanders' health, but the health of his candidacy was on their mind before that. They recognize that Elizabeth Warren was beginning to overtake him and that it seemed like the conventional wisdom was moving in that direction and they need to stop that.",
